at berlin gap on the sussex coast the  sea continues its relentless march  coastal erosion has long been a  challenge here but climate change is  accelerating the damage  kate and graham harris stayed in a hotel  on the cliff top for their honeymoon  more than 30 years ago it's much closer  to the ocean now than it was back then  i guess you realize that it is eroding  and that you know we are losing  our landscape because it is disappearing  into the sea and actually in you don't  imagine that you see it in your lifetime  but this isn't just a local problem  in england alone there are more than  five million homes and properties at  risk from coastal river or surface water  flooding and as sea levels rise and  extreme weather becomes more frequent  the number of people in harm's way is  likely to increase  at the moment according to data from the  environment agency hull is among the  most at-risk areas for coastal erosion  it's been estimated that up to 58 meters  of shoreline could be lost over the next  20 years if no action is taken to  prevent it and elsewhere in england up  to 40 meters could be lost in places  such as shoreham by sea lansing worthing  and liverpool  the threat has prompted this stark  warning  in the long term  climate change means that some of our  communities both in this country and  around the world  cannot stay where they are  it is simply too early to predict which  communities will be affected many of  course will be kept safe by flood  defenses and other adaptations but some  places like fairborn in wales have  already been told that the council can't  hold back the water forever  there is unfortunately not enough  funding to go around to protect  communities from flooding and coastal  erosion everywhere and so the importance  is about having that early and honest  conversation and working with  communities to make sure that their  voices are heard  despite billions of pounds in planned  spending on flooding the government has  always known there will be a limit to  how much it can do  the forces driven by climate change far  too powerful  hannah thomas peter sky news in sussex
 

